  "narrative": "I interpreted all signed estimates relative to the pre-registered hypothesis direction: a positive signed estimate supports the registered hypothesis, and a negative signed estimate opposes it. To judge the 10% clinical threshold, I used the absolute overall mean of each dependency outcome as the baseline scale, so approximately: KRAS threshold ≈0.0245, BRAF ≈0.0248, EGFR ≈0.0250, ERBB2 ≈0.0260, NFE2L2 ≈0.0093, MDM2 ≈0.0269, SMARCA2 ≈0.0415, POLR2A ≈0.0255, USP7 ≈0.0093, WRN ≈0.0301, KIF18A ≈0.0256. H1 has a positive signed estimate of 0.0137 (about 5.6%), below threshold, but its interval reaches the threshold, so the overall KRAS effect is unresolved and deserves subgroup follow-up. H2 is negative/opposite and not clinically meaningful, so the overall BRAF mutation hypothesis is rejected. H3 is exactly borderline for significance (0.0252, about 10.1%), with a 95% interval spanning nearly zero, so it is scientifically interesting but statistically and multiplicity-fragile; I keep it unresolved and propose adenocarcinoma follow-up. H4 is null-like and rejected. H5 strongly opposes the registered direction: after reversing direction, KEAP1 loss is associated with a much less negative NFE2L2 dependency score by about 0.796, far above the outcome-specific 10% threshold; I reject the original hypothesis but register the opposite-direction claim as an accepted in-dataset discovery and propose a batch-interaction audit because a very large effect could be technical or context dependent. H6 has small positive support for TP53 loss weakening MDM2 dependency, but the interval includes zero and only reaches threshold at the upper end, so it is unresolved and I propose a lineage-subtype refinement. H7 is a strong accepted effect: SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, well beyond threshold with a very tight positive interval. H8 is small, opposite to hypothesis, and not clinically meaningful, so the overall ARID1A-loss hypothesis is rejected. H9 is far below threshold and rejected. H12 is a very strong accepted effect: TP53 loss is associated with much weaker USP7 dependency, about 0.802 on the signed direction, far above threshold; however this is surprising and potentially dataset- or context-dependent, so I request one independent validation for H12. H13 is strongly accepted: MSI-high is associated with much stronger WRN dependency, about 0.790, consistent with synthetic-lethality-like behavior, and I propose adenocarcinoma follow-up. H14 is small and not meaningful, so the RB1-loss KIF18A hypothesis is rejected. All conclusions are associations in this supplied dataset and do not establish causality or generalizability without validation.",
  "research_notes": "First-wave results: strong in-dataset accepted effects for SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency, TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency, and MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency. A large but opposite effect was found for KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency, so I registered and initially accepted the reversed direction while keeping a batch-interaction audit. Weak/null overall effects: KRAS-BRAF-EGFR-ERBB2 oncogene dependency axes, TP53 loss weakening MDM2, ARID1A loss increasing SMARCA2, MYC amplification increasing POLR2A dependency, RB1 loss increasing KIF18A. Next priorities: (1) independent validation of H12/USP7 due to surprising magnitude and possible technical or confounded origin; (2) subgroup refinements for borderline or diluted effects: KRAS dependency in adenocarcinoma, EGFR dependency in adenocarcinoma, MDM2 effect by p53 status in adenocarcinoma, WRN effect in adenocarcinoma; (3) specificity/interaction audits for strong mechanistic effects, especially SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 interaction with ARID1A loss and KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 batch interaction; (4) deferred claims H10/H11/H15/H16/H17 can be prioritized only if MYC or oncogene cross-talk refinements appear important. Keep multiplicity discipline: only run analyses that resolve borderline effects, audit large effects, or test specificity of accepted discoveries.",
